WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:06.840
Okay, let's keep moving to the next use case and we have the following task.

00:06.840 --> 00:12.480
It says assign unique IDs to the rows of the table orders archive.

00:12.760 --> 00:17.640
So now guys, we might be in a situation where you have a table without any primary key and you would

00:17.640 --> 00:19.760
like to create an ID for each row.

00:19.800 --> 00:26.480
So in order to do that, we can use the function row number in order to generate unique identifier IDs

00:26.680 --> 00:28.200
for each row inside our table.

00:28.200 --> 00:33.240
If we don't have one, and generating such ID for each row, it's very important to do stuff like importing

00:33.240 --> 00:39.640
data, exporting data, maybe joining tables as well using this ID, or let's say optimizing the performance

00:39.640 --> 00:41.360
of query using the ID.

00:41.360 --> 00:44.720
So now let's see how we can generate that using row number okay.

00:44.760 --> 00:49.200
So now let's first select the table order archive in order to understand the content.

00:49.200 --> 00:53.920
So select star from Sales Orders archive.

00:54.280 --> 00:55.960
So let's go and execute.

00:55.960 --> 00:59.000
So now by checking the result you can see that we have ten orders.

00:59.160 --> 01:02.000
And we have repetitions in the order ID over here.

01:02.000 --> 01:03.920
So it is not really a primary key.

01:03.960 --> 01:07.000
As you can see over here we have twice the ID four.

01:07.000 --> 01:09.320
And here we have three times the ID six.

01:09.320 --> 01:13.600
So now what we're going to do we're going to go and generate unique identifier for each row.

01:13.800 --> 01:18.200
So in order to do that what we're going to do we're going to go over here and say row number.

01:18.960 --> 01:21.520
And then we're going to define the window function.

01:21.520 --> 01:29.410
We don't partition the data at all But we have to sort the data by the order ID, so order by order

01:29.410 --> 01:33.610
ID or you can use something else as well using the order date or something.

01:33.650 --> 01:34.490
Doesn't matter.

01:34.730 --> 01:37.810
So let's add to it order date as well.

01:38.250 --> 01:41.490
And let's call it unique ID.

01:41.530 --> 01:44.770
Let's go and execute this now by checking the data.

01:44.770 --> 01:49.210
You can see that we have a new ID over here that comes from the row number.

01:49.490 --> 01:51.810
And we have like a unique identifier.

01:51.850 --> 01:53.330
As you can see we have ten rows.

01:53.370 --> 01:57.610
And with that we have as well ten different distinct unique IDs.

01:57.610 --> 01:59.730
So with this as you can see we have solved the task.

01:59.730 --> 02:04.530
And we have now a unique identifier and ID for the table orders archive.

02:04.570 --> 02:09.370
So now having this ID we can do many stuff like joining tables or doing something special and important

02:09.370 --> 02:11.010
called Paginating.

02:11.170 --> 02:15.130
Imagine we have like a huge table and we would like to retrieve the data.

02:15.130 --> 02:21.370
So now in order to not have all the data in one go, we can go and divide the data by the primary ID

02:21.370 --> 02:22.730
or by unique identifier.

02:22.730 --> 02:29.400
For example, we can make a page from one until 100,000, and then the second page starts from 100 to

02:29.400 --> 02:29.850
200.

02:30.370 --> 02:34.930
So now by dividing the data we can maybe improve exporting or importing data.

02:35.250 --> 02:37.530
Or we can have faster retrieval for the users.

02:37.530 --> 02:40.730
We don't want to have the whole data in one go in one page.

02:40.730 --> 02:43.210
So it has a lot of benefits using Paginating.

02:43.210 --> 02:46.770
And we can do that only if we have a nice idea like this.
